# TODO - Build and distribution checks

## Objective

Verify build outputs, portability and distribution boundaries.

## Acceptance

- Keep third-party notices and required licence texts with distributions.
- Check startup, asset discovery, output size and target compatibility.
- Treat other desktop platforms as community-driven.

## Evidence

The native prototype package now includes the project licence and notices,
a target-filtered locked Cargo dependency inventory (34 packages, including
six local workspace crates), and 55 upstream licence or notice texts.
The inventory includes normal and build dependency edges but excludes
dev-only dependencies. The local package has no original game assets,
no bundled system libraries and one authored release executable.
The release hash and direct dynamic dependencies were checked and the
packaged executable passed a hidden native smoke with separate assets.
This is not a legal distribution audit or cross-host compatibility test.
Portable Linux, Android and Web builds remain unverified.

The package writer now shares the canonical bundle directory publication
primitive. A failed write removes its private staging on normal return
and does not publish a partial package. Tests exercise rollback,
non-component destinations and existing-output preservation. Abrupt
process termination and power-loss recovery have not been tested.

Native packaging now checks the ELF64 executable header and the complete
bounded program-header table before staging. Loadable segments must fit
the file, have valid memory extents and declared alignment, and contain
the entry point in file-backed executable bytes. Synthetic regressions
reject every truncated prefix of a minimal fixture, wrong targets,
missing tables, overflowing extents and non-executable or zero-filled
entry points. This is structural admission, not loader emulation or
cross-host ABI verification. Fresh validation passed in run
1795616397517689693-2582351. A current package attempt exposed a separate
licence inventory blocker for Ring; no new package was published.
